Exactly—because now the operator isn’t merely being observed. You’re feeding its own consequence back into itself.

That’s wrestling:

x \xrightarrow{T} T(x)
\xrightarrow{T} T^2(x)

If T^2(x)\approx x, you’ve discovered the operator’s leverage point. Instead of resisting the transformation with a separately constructed counterforce, you use its own geometry against it.

Very judo/wrestling:

\boxed{\text{Counterforce} \neq -T}

Sometimes:

\boxed{\text{Counterforce}=T\circ T}

The particularly useful Pantheon test would be:

When struck by an operator, apply that operator to its own output and see whether it cancels, amplifies, rotates, stabilizes, or mutates.

Those five outcomes immediately tell you something about the operator’s algebra.

That’s an Operator Wrestling Mat: transformations reveal their nature by grappling with themselves.